00001302f401e803050000001402f40100000500000013020000a005e80300000000430000000000}}{
\par Advanced User Features:
\par }{\i The following are not supported by Activision
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {How to make a Call To Power scenario
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Requirements
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Civilization: Call to Power v1.2. \line A knowledge of how to create folders. \line The ability to create
 and edit text files. 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 1: Directories
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Every scenario is part of a scenario pack. Every scenario pack has its own folder in the }{\i\fs20 Scenarios}{\fs20 
 subdirectory of CTP. For example, if CTP is installed to }{\i\fs20 C:\\Program Files\\Activision\\Civilization-Call To Power\\}{\fs20 , and you have applied patch 1.2, there should be a folder named }{\i\fs20 C:\\Program Files\\Activision\\
Civilization-Call To Power\\Scenarios}{\fs20 . Inside this folder, you will see a directory named }{\i\fs20 Official}{\fs20 . This is where the scenarios Activision created reside. In order to
 make your own scenarios, you need to first create your own scenario pack directory. For this example, we'll call the directory }{\i\fs20 MyScenarioPack}{\fs20 . Make it now. The full path, if you've installed to }{\i\fs20 C:\\Program Files\\Activision\\
Civilization-Call To Power\\}{\fs20  should be }{\i\fs20 C:\\Program Files\\Activision\\Civilization-Call To Power\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\}{\fs20 . 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 From here on out, }{\i\fs20 C:\\Program Files\\Activision\\Civilization-Call To Power}{\fs20 
 will be represented by }{\i\fs20 ...}{\fs20  You should replace }{\i\fs20 ...}{\fs20  with the directory where you installed Civilization: Call To Power. 
\par \~Now that you have a folder for your scenario pack, each scenario needs to have a folder. These have to be named }{\i\fs20 scen}{\b\i\fs20 xxxx}{\fs20 , where }{\b\i\fs20 xxxx}{\fs20  is replaced with a number, starting from }{\i\fs20 0000}{\fs20 
 and moving up incrementally. Make a folder for your first scenario now, name it }{\i\fs20 scen0000}{\fs20 . The full path from the main scenarios directory should be }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000}{\fs20 . 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 2: Description files
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Every scenario pack needs a description of the pack. The name of this file is always packlist.
txt, and resides in the main pack folder, so it will be }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\packlist.txt}{\fs20 
 in this example. packlist.txt is a plain text file that always contains 3 lines: The name of the scenario pack, a description of the scenario pack, and the number of scenarios in the pack. For our example, create the file }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\
MyScenarioPack\\packlist.txt}{\fs20  now and enter these three lines: 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\i\fs20 packlist.txt:}{\fs20  
\par }\trowd \trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8162 \cellx8102\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl
\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {My Scenario Pack
\par This is a description of my scenario pack.\~ My scenario pack rocks.
\par 1\cell }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \trowd \trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone 
\clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8162 \cellx8102\row }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
Every individual scenario needs a description file too. The name of this file is }{\i\fs20 scenario.txt}{\fs20  and goes in the }{\i\fs20 scen}{\b\i\fs20 xxxx}{\fs20 
 folder for the scenario. Scenario.txt consists of only two lines, the name of the scenario, and a description of the scenario. }{\b\fs20 Remember the name you use here, it will be important later.}{\fs20 
 Create this file for your first scenario now, it should be named }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\scenario.txt}{\fs20 . Put these lines in the file: 
\par }{\b\i\fs20 scenario.txt:}{\fs20  
\par }\trowd \trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth7562 \cellx7502\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl
\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {My First Scenario
\par This is a description of my first scenario.\~ I like scenarios.\cell }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \trowd 
\trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth7562 \cellx7502\row }\pard\plain 
\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
This says that your scenario's name is "My First Scenario". You will need to type it exactly the same later when we get to saved games. 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 3: Check it out
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 That's the bare minimum needed to 
create a scenario. This scenario won't do anything interesting yet, it will be exactly like playing a normal game. But go into the game anyway and see if it shows up. If you run the game and go to Single Player->New Game->Select Scenario, you should see a
 
new scenario pack named "My Scenario Pack". Double clicking on it, or clicking and selectiong OK, should open the pack, and you should a scenario named "My First Scenario" inside. Double clicking this should take you back to the single player screen, whic
h
 should now say "Scenario: My First Scenario" underneath the "Standard Game" button (which was the "Select Scenario" button before) If you don't see your scenario pack or your scenario, go back and check the steps above to make sure everything is in the r
ight place. To summarize, here are the files and folders you should have created at this point: 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {...
\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\
\par ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\packlist.txt
\par ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\
\par ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\scenario.txt
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 4: Make a map
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
Launch the map editor. Create a map you want to use for your scenario. For this example, press the "Start Locs." button on the editor window, and select "Start Loc. by Civ". Place starting locations for the first four civs, Greeks, Ame
ricans, Scots, and Irish. Now click "Save Scenario As". In the first dialog that appears, enter }{\b\fs20 My First Scenario}{\fs20 , which must match exactly what you entered as the first line of }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\
scenario.txt}{\fs20 . Click ok. On the next scre
en, which looks like a normal save game dialog, enter a folder name in which to save the game in the first text field ("Game:"). For this example, use the folder name "NewScen". This folder will be placed in ...\\ctp_program\\save\\games\\
NewScen. In the second text field ("Save:") enter "savegame.csg". This is the filename that will be used to save the file inside the }{\i\fs20 NewScen}{\fs20  folder. You can use another name, but when you move the file to the actual scenario folder, it }
{\b\fs20 must}{\fs20  be named savegame.csg, so it makes sense to name it that now. \line At this point, you should have this file: \line }{\i\fs20 ...\\ctp_program\\save\\games\\NewScen\\savegame.csg}{\fs20  \line 
You should also have a scenario directory from step 1 at: \line }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\}{\fs20  
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 In order to make your newly saved map 
the map that is used for "My First Scenario", you just need to move }{\i\fs20 ...\\ctp_program\\save\\games\\NewScen\\savegame.csg}{\fs20  to }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\savegame.csg}{\fs20 
. Do that now. The complete list of files you've added under ...\\scenarios should now look like this: 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {...
\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\\line ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\packlist.txt\line ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\\line ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\scenario.txt\line ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\savegame.csg
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 5: Check it out
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
Start up the game again. Go to Single Player->New Game->Select Scenario. Open "My Scenario Pack". Open "My First Scenario". This time, instead of going back to the normal new game screen, you should be presented with a series of dialogs. The first is for 
n
umber of players. 3 or 4 should be the only legal choices (since you placed 4 starting locations in step 4). Choose 4. Next you should be asked to choose a civilization. The only legal choices should be the civilizations for which you placed starting loca
t
ions. Finally, you should get the difficulty dialog. Choose the difficulty you want. Once the game has loaded, open the cheat tool and click Show Map. Verify that the game is using the map you saved and that there are 4 settlers in the places where you pu
t starting locations. If you have that, you've now created a real scenario with a fixed map and fixed starting locations that uses the standard rules, congratulations! 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 6: Change the rules
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Every scenario pack can override the standard text files. Every
 scenario can also override it's pack's text files. For this example, we'll override the standard files for just scen0000 ("My First Scenario"). 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \~Create these new folders \line }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\}{\fs20  
\line }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\gamedata\\}{\fs20  \line Any file you place in }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\gamedata\\}{\fs20  will now be read }{\b\fs20 instead of}{\fs20 
 the standard file in }{\i\fs20 ...\\ctp_data\\default\\gamedata\\}{\fs20 . So, let's copy the units.txt from }{\i\fs20 ...\\ctp_data\\default\\gamedata\\units.txt}{\fs20  to }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\gamedata\\units.txt
}{\fs20  Now let's make settlers look like cows: 
\par Open up }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\gamedata\\units.txt}{\fs20 . \line Find the unit that starts with "UNIT_SETTLER". Look through UNIT_SETTLER's description until you come to 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
DEFAULT_SPRITE\~\~\~ SPRITE_SETTLER
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Change that line to say 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
DEFAULT_SPRITE\~\~\~ SPRITE_CATTLE
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
Now go back and repeat Step 5. This time when you start the game, you should see cows instead of settlers. They will still behave as settlers, all you did was change the sprite. But this gives you an idea of how to start modifying units on your own. 

\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
Ok, so you know how to change the standard rules. What about creating your own rules? SLIC (documented elsewhere) allows you to modify the game's behavior with triggers on various game events. To add SLIC code to a scenario, you need only create the file 
}{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\gamedata\\scenario.slc}{\fs20  and place any SLIC code you want in it. To test things out, create the file now, and place this code in it: 
\par }{\b\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\default\\gamedata\\scenario.slc:}{\fs20  
\par }\trowd \trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8162 \cellx8102\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl
\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
trigger 'NewWarrior' when (city.built && IsHumanPlayer(g.player)) \{
\par \~ CreateUnit(g.player, UnitType("UNIT_WARRIOR"), city.location, 0);
\par \~ Message(g.player, 'NewWarriorMessage');
\par \}
\par 
\par messagebox 'NewWarriorMessage' \{
\par \~\~\~ Text(ID_NEW_WARRIOR_MESSAGE);
\par \}\cell }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \trowd \trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone 
\clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8162 \cellx8102\row }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
This code contains a reference to a string that's not in the standard string files. So you also need to create a scenario strings file. You should place this in }{\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\english\\gamedata\\scen_str.txt}{\fs20 
. You will need to create the "english" and "english\\gamedata" directories. Note that if your copy of CTP is in another language, you need to use that language as the first director
y name instead of "english". And if you want to include translations for other languages with your scenario, you can create all of the directories for which you have translations and put a scen_str.txt in each of them. Anyway, assuming you're doing this w
ith an english version, put this in your new strings file: 
\par }{\b\i\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\english\\gamedata\\scen_str.txt:}{\fs20  
\par }\trowd \trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8642 \cellx8582\pard\plain \s16\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl
\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
NEW_WARRIOR_MESSAGE "You have received a free warrior in [city.1.name]"\cell }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \trowd 
\trgaph15\trleft-60\trftsWidth1\trpaddl15\trpaddr15\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrnone \clbrdrl\brdrnone \clbrdrb\brdrnone \clbrdrr\brdrnone \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth8642 \cellx8582\row }\pard\plain 
\s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
Now if you go back and repeat step 5, every time you build a city with one of your settlers (err, cows), you should receive a free warrior in that city and a message telling you about the free warrior. 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 7: Custom Icons
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 This is optional, but allows you
 to jazz up your Scenario Pack and Scenarios with nice little graphics for the Scenario Picker interface. 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 
If you like, you can override the default Scenario Pack and Scenario icons that are displayed in the picker screen inside the game.\~ This is easy to do.\~
 All you have to do is make 16 bit TARGA images, name them the right name, and put them in the right place(s). 
\par }{\i\fs20 Scenario Pack Icon}{\fs20  
\par This is a 16 bit TARGA image of size 150x90 pixels.\~ It's called: 
\par }{\cs17\f2\fs20 packicon.tga}{\fs20  
\par Using the above example scenario, put this TGA file here: 
\par }{\cs17\f2\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\packicon.tga}{\fs20  
\par }{\i\fs20 Scenario Icon}{\fs20  
\par This is also a 16 bit TARGA image of size 150x90 pixels.\~ It's called: 
\par }{\cs17\f2\fs20 scenicon.tga}{\fs20  
\par Using the above example scenario, put this TGA file here: 
\par }{\cs17\f2\fs20 ...\\scenarios\\MyScenarioPack\\scen0000\\scenicon.tga}{\fs20  
\par That's all you need to do.\~ The images have to be in the exact format specified (16 bit TARGA images, not 24 or 32 bit ones,) named correctly, and placed in the right directory to show up in the game. 
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Step 8: Have fun!
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 Now you know ever
ything you need to know to make a scenario. To add more scenarios to your scenario pack, all you have to do is name the folders scen0001, scen0002, etc., and update the number in packlist.txt to reflect the number of scenarios you've created. 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 That's it. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {AI taking over PBEM/Hotseat player
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \~
You can use the /attach <player#> command from the chat window (hit the ' key) to assign an AI to play the turn of a Hotseat player that has left the game.\~ To allow a human player to assume an AI players turn, use the /detach <player#> command.\~
 For PBEM games, you must\~set all players as\~ human (and include an email address) for the first turn of play.\~ On any further turns you can then attach/detach AI's as before. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Registry location for .csg files
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \~HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\\.csg and HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\\csgfile 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs27\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Adding Governments in Scenarios
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 \~
If a scenario maker wants to modify the default CTP governments (described in the govern.txt file) then there are two ways to do this.\~ The first is to modify one of the 12 original governments in the govern.txt file.\~
 You can modified any aspect of these governments, including their names.\~ However, you must continue to refer to them by the original name if you modify their rank ordering in the set_govern.fli file. 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\fs20 For example, if you re
define the 12th government in the govern.txt file (GOVERNMENT_VIRTUAL_DEMOCRACY), then you can make this the first choice of the AI when it is available by setting the gov_virtual_d_1 output variable in the set_govern.fli file.\~
 Any governments that you cr
eate over and above the original twelve do not have ranking variables associated with them in the fli system and will use a utility computation to decide when the new government becomes available if it is better than the one selected by the rank system in
 set_govern.\~ To force only particular AI's to use one of your additional governments you can not use the ranking system and so must instead tie the government to an advance that only certain AI personalities research, or use some similar method. \line 
\~ 
\par }}